# Rust build output (any workspace, including sundials_rs/)
target/

# LaTeX intermediates (the .tex sources and .pdf outputs ARE tracked).
# Anchored to the repo root ON PURPOSE: an unanchored `*.out` would also
# swallow sundials_rs/localref/*.out — the byte-exact C reference outputs
# the example-verification harness compares against.
/*.aux
/*.log
/*.toc
/*.out

# Pre-modification file backups (see CLAUDE.md workflow)
.backups/

# Runtime artifact produced by running the notebooks/examples
session.posim

# Python bytecode from the index tooling in tools/. The repo had no
# committed Python packages until the index landed, so nothing had ever
# needed this rule — and `git add -A` duly swept a .pyc into the merge.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Editor / OS clutter
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
# Local-only reference trees: the SolveIt 2002 C/C++/Fortran sources and
# the 2026 C++23 upgrade. These must NEVER be committed — they carry
# Numerical Recipes (not redistributable), GSL-derived (GPL-3.0) and
# GIAC (GPL-2.0) code. See CLEANROOM_PROVENANCE.md.
#
# NOTE the leading `/`, not `./`. A pattern starting `./` is anchored and
# matches a directory literally named `.`, so it silently ignores
# NOTHING — that exact mistake published 749 of these files. Trailing `/`
# restricts the match to directories.
/obsolete_or_historic/
/SolveIt/
/SolveIt_2026_MFC/

# Generated by `QM ANIMATE` in dynamic_notebooks/tunneling.posim
/scatter.html
/double_slit.html
/vol3d.html
/iso3d.html

# Stage transcripts. NOT to be published: they are an internal record.
# Leading `/`, not `./` — see the note above; `./Stages_output` would
# ignore nothing. certify_clean.sh fails if anything here is tracked.
/Stages_output/

# Films written BY the stage notebooks when they run (regenerated on demand).
StageNbks/out_*.html

# Scratch recovery artifact. Its content is a tracked example target,
# posim/examples/scene_server_reference.rs — a loose copy must never
# shadow it or get pushed alongside it.
recover.txt

# PHASE 1 refined-prompt artifact (working document, never published)
/prompt_01.md

# Scratch file the Tier-B verifier writes to compile the doc snippets
# (tools/verify_tierb_examples.py). It is regenerated on every run and
# must never be committed: as a posim example target it would be built
# by `cargo build --all-targets` and could shadow a real example.
posim/examples/_tierb_compile_check.rs

# The vendored engine's own build output. `target/` above is unanchored
# and already covers it; this line is here so the rule is visible when
# someone reads the file looking for sundials_rs.
sundials_rs/target/

# Out-of-source CMake build trees for recorder/
/build/
/recorder/build/

# JupyterLab per-session checkpoints (notebooks/ is served live)
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Upstream C reference trees for the rebound_rust / reboundx_rust
# bit-identity verification (REBOUND 5.1.1 and REBOUNDx 5.1.0, GPL).
# Reference-only, never committed — the same rule as the SUNDIALS C tree.
# Recreate with:
#   git clone https://github.com/hannorein/rebound.git rebound/rebound
#   (cd rebound/rebound && git checkout dad5f97806ecbb408dcaff728851c64e67f9f6eb)
#   git clone https://github.com/dtamayo/reboundx.git reboundx
/rebound/
/reboundx/

# planet_Mercury regenerable data (~270 MB of CSV/SQLite the two notebooks
# rebuild deterministically) and its optional JupyterLab venv. Root-level
# rules on purpose: the sub-project itself carries no .gitignore files.
/planet_Mercury/data/
/planet_Mercury/notebook/.venv/

# SQLite write-ahead-log side files. The sidecar checkpoints on close; a
# -wal/-shm pair on disk is transient state, never a dataset.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite3-wal
*.sqlite3-shm

# The encyclopedia's per-topic JupyterLab checkpoints and Python caches
SolveIt_Notebooks_for_rust/**/.ipynb_checkpoints/

# The C-reference comparison harnesses are BUILD PRODUCTS: on Windows they
# are porttest/*.exe (already ignored above via the rebound folders' own
# rules); the macOS edition had committed its arm64 Mach-O binaries under
# these extensionless names, which are useless on any other platform. Each
# harness is rebuilt from its .c source by the documented commands.
rebound_rust/porttest/addfmt_test
rebound_rust/porttest/archive_test
rebound_rust/porttest/bs_pow_diff
rebound_rust/porttest/derivatives_test
rebound_rust/porttest/frequency_test
rebound_rust/porttest/integrators_test
rebound_rust/porttest/kepler_rectilinear_c
rebound_rust/porttest/libm_diff
rebound_rust/porttest/movetocom_var_c
rebound_rust/porttest/movetocom_var_test
rebound_rust/porttest/problem_test
reboundx_rust/porttest/rebx_binary_read_c
reboundx_rust/porttest/rebx_binary_roundtrip_c
reboundx_rust/porttest/tides_spin_kozai_c
reboundx_rust/porttest/tides_spin_migration_c
reboundx_rust/porttest/tides_spin_pseudo_c
